1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,393
<font color="#000000">Comparing unpaired samples of a population measured
at different points in time is a core challenge in biology,</font>

2
00:00:07,473 --> 00:00:10,031
<font color="#000000">where measuring cells often destroys them.</font>

3
00:00:10,111 --> 00:00:14,240
<font color="#000000">Optimal transport can recover how samples map
across distributions,</font>

4
00:00:14,320 --> 00:00:16,941
<font color="#000000">but classical OT assumes mass is conserved,</font>

5
00:00:17,021 --> 00:00:22,406
<font color="#000000">which breaks down when populations grow or shrink
through cell proliferation and death.</font>

6
00:00:22,486 --> 00:00:24,165
<font color="#000000">This paper introduces NubOT,</font>

7
00:00:24,245 --> 00:00:29,567
<font color="#000000">a neural unbalanced optimal transport method built
on the formalism of semi-couplings,</font>

8
00:00:29,647 --> 00:00:34,027
<font color="#000000">letting it explicitly account for the creation and
destruction of mass.</font>

9
00:00:34,107 --> 00:00:36,729
<font color="#000000">Through a cycle-consistent training scheme,</font>

10
00:00:36,809 --> 00:00:40,686
<font color="#000000">NubOT learns both where cells move and how their
mass rescales,</font>

11
00:00:40,766 --> 00:00:45,774
<font color="#000000">and when applied to forecasting how cancer cell
lines respond to dozens of drugs,</font>

12
00:00:45,854 --> 00:00:52,810
<font color="#000000">it outperforms prior neural OT methods while
recovering biologically meaningful proliferation and death signals.</font>

13
00:00:53,190 --> 00:00:59,703
<font color="#000000">A recurring problem in the natural sciences is
modeling how a population changes after an intervention,</font>

14
00:00:59,783 --> 00:01:04,503
<font color="#000000">when you can only observe unpaired snapshots rather
than track individuals.</font>

15
00:01:04,583 --> 00:01:09,688
<font color="#000000">In single-cell biology this is unavoidable, because
profiling a cell destroys it,</font>

16
00:01:09,768 --> 00:01:12,569
<font color="#000000">so the same cell can never be measured twice.</font>

17
00:01:12,649 --> 00:01:18,137
<font color="#000000">Optimal transport offers a principled way to infer
these correspondences by learning an</font>

18
00:01:18,217 --> 00:01:20,634
<font color="#000000">optimal coupling between distributions.</font>

19
00:01:20,714 --> 00:01:24,346
<font color="#000000">But the standard formulation assumes conservation of mass,</font>

20
00:01:24,426 --> 00:01:28,443
<font color="#000000">meaning every unit of source mass must be
transported somewhere.</font>

21
00:01:28,523 --> 00:01:32,091
<font color="#000000">That assumption is violated exactly when it matters most,</font>

22
00:01:32,171 --> 00:01:35,548
<font color="#000000">in unbalanced settings where cells proliferate or die,</font>

23
00:01:35,628 --> 00:01:39,260
<font color="#000000">and the total population size shifts between measurements.</font>

24
00:01:39,640 --> 00:01:46,594
<font color="#000000">Cellular responses to drugs are highly
heterogeneous: different cell types and states can respond in opposite ways,</font>

25
00:01:46,674 --> 00:01:48,796
<font color="#000000">some proliferating while others die.</font>

26
00:01:48,876 --> 00:01:52,955
<font color="#000000">Capturing this requires nonlinear maps at the level
of single cells,</font>

27
00:01:53,035 --> 00:01:54,362
<font color="#000000">not aggregate averages.</font>

28
00:01:54,442 --> 00:01:57,971
<font color="#000000">Prior neural optimal transport methods can learn
such maps,</font>

29
00:01:58,051 --> 00:02:02,375
<font color="#000000">but they either assume the balanced setting where
mass is conserved, or,</font>

30
00:02:02,455 --> 00:02:05,433
<font color="#000000">like the state-of-the-art unbalanced GAN approach,</font>

31
00:02:05,513 --> 00:02:10,816
<font color="#000000">they only capture the general trend of growth and
shrinkage without recovering the exact</font>

32
00:02:10,896 --> 00:02:13,079
<font color="#000000">reweighting each subpopulation needs.</font>

33
00:02:13,159 --> 00:02:18,279
<font color="#000000">What has been missing is a method that jointly and
accurately models where mass moves</font>

34
00:02:18,358 --> 00:02:20,909
<font color="#000000">and how much of it is created or destroyed,</font>

35
00:02:20,989 --> 00:02:23,600
<font color="#000000">in a way that stays faithful to the biology.</font>

36
00:02:23,980 --> 00:02:27,164
<font color="#000000">The paper makes three main contributions.</font>

37
00:02:27,244 --> 00:02:30,428
<font color="#000000">First,</font>

38
00:02:30,508 --> 00:02:33,692
<font color="#000000">it introduces a novel formulation of the unbalanced
optimal transport problem that connects</font>

39
00:02:33,772 --> 00:02:36,956
<font color="#000000">the rigorous theory of semi-couplings,</font>

40
00:02:37,036 --> 00:02:40,220
<font color="#000000">which allow mass to vary,</font>

41
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:43,484
<font color="#000000">with a practical and scalable optimal-transport
mapping estimator.</font>

42
00:02:43,564 --> 00:02:46,748
<font color="#000000">Second,</font>

43
00:02:46,828 --> 00:02:50,012
<font color="#000000">it derives a computationally feasible
implementation based on dual potentials parameterized</font>

44
00:02:50,092 --> 00:02:53,276
<font color="#000000">by input convex neural networks,</font>

45
00:02:53,356 --> 00:02:56,540
<font color="#000000">together with learned reweighting functions that
predict mass changes.</font>

46
00:02:56,620 --> 00:02:59,804
<font color="#000000">Third,</font>

47
00:02:59,884 --> 00:03:03,068
<font color="#000000">it proposes a new cycle-consistent training
procedure that alternates between updating</font>

48
00:03:03,148 --> 00:03:06,332
<font color="#000000">these maps and rescaling functions,</font>

49
00:03:06,412 --> 00:03:09,596
<font color="#000000">and crucially generalizes to new, out-of-sample cells.</font>

50
00:03:09,676 --> 00:03:12,860
<font color="#000000">Together these give the first neural method that
estimates semi-couplings for unbalanced OT at single-cell scale.</font>

51
00:03:13,240 --> 00:03:18,733
<font color="#000000">NubOT's key idea is to split the hard unbalanced
problem into two coupled pieces: feature</font>

52
00:03:18,813 --> 00:03:20,862
<font color="#000000">transformation and mass rescaling.</font>

53
00:03:20,942 --> 00:03:26,372
<font color="#000000">It introduces proxy measures that are simply
rescaled versions of the source and target,</font>

54
00:03:26,452 --> 00:03:31,507
<font color="#000000">weighted by scalar fields eta and zeta, chosen so
the two proxies have equal mass.</font>

55
00:03:31,587 --> 00:03:36,704
<font color="#000000">Between these now-balanced measures it solves a
standard optimal transport problem,</font>

56
00:03:36,784 --> 00:03:42,340
<font color="#000000">learning forward and backward Monge maps as the
gradients of convex potentials represented</font>

57
00:03:42,420 --> 00:03:44,343
<font color="#000000">by input convex neural networks.</font>

58
00:03:44,423 --> 00:03:49,040
<font color="#000000">Because the marginals themselves shift as the
reweighting functions update,</font>

59
00:03:49,120 --> 00:03:54,613
<font color="#000000">NubOT uses an alternating minimization: it
estimates the mass rescaling with an efficient</font>

60
00:03:54,693 --> 00:03:57,055
<font color="#000000">single-step unbalanced Sinkhorn update,</font>

61
00:03:57,135 --> 00:04:01,187
<font color="#000000">then updates the transport potentials through a
min-max objective,</font>

62
00:04:01,267 --> 00:04:02,690
<font color="#000000">cycling between the two.</font>

63
00:04:02,770 --> 00:04:06,259
<font color="#000000">The reweighting functions are learned as neural networks,</font>

64
00:04:06,339 --> 00:04:13,210
<font color="#000000">so at test time the model can map a new cell and
simultaneously predict whether its mass should grow or shrink.</font>

65
00:04:13,590 --> 00:04:17,673
<font color="#000000">The method is evaluated on two settings.</font>

66
00:04:17,753 --> 00:04:21,836
<font color="#000000">The first is a synthetic benchmark: a
two-dimensional mixture of three Gaussian clusters,</font>

67
00:04:21,916 --> 00:04:25,999
<font color="#000000">where the target keeps the same clusters but
changes their proportions and shifts them in space,</font>

68
00:04:26,079 --> 00:04:30,162
<font color="#000000">across three scenarios of increasing imbalance.</font>

69
00:04:30,242 --> 00:04:34,325
<font color="#000000">The second is a real single-cell perturbation dataset,</font>

70
00:04:34,405 --> 00:04:38,488
<font color="#000000">generated with the imaging technology called 4i,</font>

71
00:04:38,568 --> 00:04:42,652
<font color="#000000">that tracks two co-cultured melanoma cell lines
responding to twenty-five different drugs.</font>

72
00:04:42,732 --> 00:04:46,815
<font color="#000000">Cells are measured at zero, eight, and twenty-four hours,</font>

73
00:04:46,895 --> 00:04:50,978
<font color="#000000">with control cells exposed only to a vehicle
serving as the source and the perturbed populations serving as targets.</font>

74
00:04:51,058 --> 00:04:55,141
<font color="#000000">The two cell lines are distinguished by mutually
exclusive protein markers,</font>

75
00:04:55,221 --> 00:04:59,304
<font color="#000000">MelA and Sox9,</font>

76
00:04:59,384 --> 00:05:03,467
<font color="#000000">and additional markers for proliferation and cell
death allow the predicted mass changes</font>

77
00:05:03,547 --> 00:05:07,630
<font color="#000000">to be checked against biology.</font>

78
00:05:08,010 --> 00:05:12,095
<font color="#000000">Across the real single-cell task, NubOT outperforms
every baseline,</font>

79
00:05:12,175 --> 00:05:15,452
<font color="#000000">including the current state-of-the-art unbalanced GAN,</font>

80
00:05:15,532 --> 00:05:18,684
<font color="#000000">in almost all of the twenty-five drug perturbations,</font>

81
00:05:18,764 --> 00:05:25,709
<font color="#000000">measured by a weighted version of kernel maximum
mean discrepancy between predicted and observed perturbed cells.</font>

82
00:05:25,789 --> 00:05:26,952
<font color="#000000">Just as importantly,</font>

83
00:05:27,032 --> 00:05:32,422
<font color="#000000">it does so while getting the mass changes right: on
the synthetic benchmark it maps each</font>

84
00:05:32,502 --> 00:05:36,649
<font color="#000000">cluster to its correct target without leaking mass
between clusters,</font>

85
00:05:36,729 --> 00:05:40,193
<font color="#000000">and it predicts the exact reweighting each cluster needs,</font>

86
00:05:40,273 --> 00:05:45,228
<font color="#000000">where the GAN baseline only captures the broad
direction of growth and shrinkage.</font>

87
00:05:45,308 --> 00:05:51,320
<font color="#000000">This combination of accurate feature mapping and
accurate mass rescaling is what sets NubOT apart.</font>

88
00:05:51,700 --> 00:05:56,005
<font color="#000000">To test whether the predicted mass changes are
biologically meaningful,</font>

89
00:05:56,085 --> 00:05:59,587
<font color="#000000">the authors compare them against independent measurements.</font>

90
00:05:59,667 --> 00:06:03,292
<font color="#000000">Summing NubOT's predicted weights within each
subpopulation,</font>

91
00:06:03,372 --> 00:06:08,789
<font color="#000000">they find a strong correlation with the observed
change in cell counts after eight hours,</font>

92
00:06:08,869 --> 00:06:11,877
<font color="#000000">with a correlation coefficient of point nine five.</font>

93
00:06:11,957 --> 00:06:15,891
<font color="#000000">After twenty-four hours the correlation drops to
point four four,</font>

94
00:06:15,971 --> 00:06:22,437
<font color="#000000">because for some drugs the induced cell death is so
severe that too few cells remain to evaluate reliably,</font>

95
00:06:22,517 --> 00:06:24,537
<font color="#000000">yet the overall trend still holds.</font>

96
00:06:24,617 --> 00:06:29,663
<font color="#000000">Going further, the predicted weights line up
spatially with a proliferation marker,</font>

97
00:06:29,743 --> 00:06:32,442
<font color="#000000">Ki67, where NubOT predicts weights above one,</font>

98
00:06:32,522 --> 00:06:36,457
<font color="#000000">and with an apoptosis marker where it predicts
weights below one,</font>

99
00:06:36,537 --> 00:06:41,891
<font color="#000000">confirming that the model is recovering real
proliferation and death signals rather than</font>

100
00:06:41,971 --> 00:06:44,300
<font color="#000000">fitting the distribution superficially.</font>

101
00:06:44,680 --> 00:06:47,132
<font color="#000000">The headline numbers tell a clear story.</font>

102
00:06:47,212 --> 00:06:54,221
<font color="#000000">NubOT beats every baseline in almost all of the
twenty-five drug perturbations on the distributional fit metric.</font>

103
00:06:54,301 --> 00:07:00,045
<font color="#000000">Its predicted per-subpopulation weights correlate
with observed cell counts at a coefficient</font>

104
00:07:00,125 --> 00:07:02,387
<font color="#000000">of point nine five after eight hours,</font>

105
00:07:02,467 --> 00:07:04,286
<font color="#000000">an extremely strong agreement,</font>

106
00:07:04,366 --> 00:07:10,173
<font color="#000000">and still at point four four after twenty-four
hours despite drug-induced cell death thinning</font>

107
00:07:10,253 --> 00:07:11,882
<font color="#000000">the observable populations.</font>

108
00:07:11,962 --> 00:07:18,212
<font color="#000000">The evaluation spans two co-cultured melanoma cell
lines distinguished by the MelA and Sox9 markers,</font>

109
00:07:18,292 --> 00:07:21,440
<font color="#000000">imaged at three time points with the 4i technology,</font>

110
00:07:21,520 --> 00:07:27,010
<font color="#000000">and compares against four baselines including the
prior state-of-the-art unbalanced GAN.</font>

111
00:07:27,390 --> 00:07:33,607
<font color="#000000">The takeaway is that unbalanced optimal transport
can be made both practical and biologically faithful.</font>

112
00:07:33,687 --> 00:07:36,848
<font color="#000000">By reformulating it through learnable semi-couplings,</font>

113
00:07:36,928 --> 00:07:42,411
<font color="#000000">and training the maps and reweighting functions
with a cycle-consistent alternating scheme,</font>

114
00:07:42,491 --> 00:07:49,503
<font color="#000000">NubOT simultaneously predicts the movement and the
creation or destruction of mass at the level of individual cells.</font>

115
00:07:49,583 --> 00:07:55,311
<font color="#000000">It outperforms previous neural optimal transport
methods on the challenging task of forecasting</font>

116
00:07:55,391 --> 00:07:57,696
<font color="#000000">how cancer cell lines respond to drugs,</font>

117
00:07:57,776 --> 00:08:03,626
<font color="#000000">and it does so while producing predictions that
align with known proliferation and death markers.</font>

118
00:08:03,706 --> 00:08:08,578
<font color="#000000">Explicitly modeling cell birth and death, rather
than assuming mass is conserved,</font>

119
00:08:08,658 --> 00:08:10,290
<font color="#000000">is what makes this possible.</font>
